## P3. The saver and the two worlds
====================================================================
```
PHASE P3: the saver ("what would have saved you") and the two-worlds comparison. Engine only, no UI. Build on src/engine (P1 runMission, P2 Storm Clock). All numbers go in rules.ts.

NEW FILE src/engine/saver.ts
Types:
  Edit = { kind: 'bolt', sol: number, cat: 'air' | 'shield' | 'lab', delta: 1 | -1 } | { kind: 'clock', block: number | 'none' }
  Saver = { edits: Edit[], cost: number, text: string, before: Result, after: Result }
Export findSaver(cfg, plan, result): Saver | null.
Rules:
- Only look for a saver if result is not 3 stars.
- Goal: if the mission failed (survived = false), find edits that make it survive. If it survived with under 3 stars, find edits that raise the star count.
- Single edits first: for every sol 1..7, every category, delta +1 or -1, result must stay within 0..3. Plus clock edits on the storm sol: shelter at block b for each b in 1..K (clock array: work before b, shelter at b), or 'none' (work every block). Then all PAIRS of two different edits (never two edits on the same sol and category).
- cost = 1 per bolt changed, 1 per clock edit. Choose the lowest cost that reaches the goal. Tie-break: fewer bolts moved, then earlier sol, then the order air, shield, lab.
- VERIFY every candidate by re-running runMission with the edited plan and the same cfg (same seed, same storm). Never claim a result you did not re-run. Compare before and after to build the text.
- text: one friendly sentence, 12 words or fewer, never blaming. Templates:
  bolt -1: 'If you had used 1 less bolt on {thing} on sol {n}, {benefit}.'
  bolt +1: 'If you had used 1 more bolt on {thing} on sol {n}, {benefit}.'
  clock block b: 'If you had gone to shelter on block {b}, the crew would have stayed safe.'
  clock none: 'If you had worked all blocks, you would have earned more science.'
  thing: air -> 'the air machine', shield -> 'the shield', lab -> 'science'.
  benefit: air failure fixed -> 'the air would have lasted'; health failure fixed -> 'the crew would have stayed safe'; stars gained -> 'you would have earned {n} stars'.
  For two edits, join the two clauses with ' and ' after the first 'If you had', and keep it as short as possible.
- If nothing works: return null. The UI will say 'This one was very hard. Try the Safe plan.'

NEW FILE src/engine/compare.ts
Export compareWorlds(a: { site: Site, result: Result }, b: { site: Site, result: Result }) returning { rows: Array<{ label: string, a: string, b: string }>, sentence: string }.
Rows: Stars, Air left, Health left, Science, Warning time (visibleHours, or 'Old news' if oldMessage).
sentence (12 words or fewer, uses real numbers), choose the first that applies:
  1. work blocks (K) differ: 'On {site} the warning came {n} minutes late, so you had {m} fewer work blocks.'
  2. one result has oldMessage true: 'On Mars the message was old news. Earth could not warn you in time.'
  3. power left differs by 10 or more: '{site} gets less sunlight, so power ran lower.'
  4. otherwise: 'Both worlds felt much the same this time.'
Use exact numbers from the results, never invented ones.

TESTS (src/engine/saver.test.ts and compare.test.ts, hand-computed values in comments)
1. Build a plan that fails on air (air 0 bolts every sol). findSaver returns a saver, and re-running its edits survives.
2. Build a plan that survives with 1 star (science too low). The saver raises it to 2 stars and the test re-runs it.
3. A 3-star run returns null.
4. A run where nothing helps returns null. Construct it by choosing a cfg where even the best single and pair edit cannot survive (for example, very low power and a big storm), or say in DECISIONS.md why you could not.
5. Tie-break order: when two edits have the same cost, the earlier sol wins.
6. The text has 12 words or fewer, and mentions the right sol number.
7. Determinism: the same inputs give the same saver.
8. Clock edit: a caught-outside run on a particle storm is saved by 'shelter at block b'.
9. compareWorlds: K differs between Moon and Mars for a short-gap storm and the sentence names the minutes and the missing blocks.
10. compareWorlds returns 5 rows with the right labels.

COMMON RULES: Only read and edit files inside this project folder. Follow AGENTS.md. Never change an assertion or an expected value in an existing test to make it pass; if a test fails for a reason you cannot fix, stop and tell me. This runs on Windows, so use cross-platform commands and path.join. Keep every file under 250 lines. Finish by pasting the full output of npm test and npm run build, listing every file you changed and every edit to an existing test, writing your choices in DECISIONS.md, updating docs/AI_USE.md, and saying what you could not check.
```

