## P5. Real NASA data
====================================================================
```
PHASE P5: real NASA data. Use the nasa-data skill. This phase needs network access. If your terminal cannot reach api.nasa.gov, say so and stop.

DATA SOURCE: NASA DONKI at https://api.nasa.gov/DONKI/ . Endpoints: /FLR (solar flares), /SEP (solar energetic particle events). Query: ?startDate=YYYY-MM-DD&endDate=YYYY-MM-DD&api_key=KEY. Use process.env.NASA_API_KEY; fall back to DEMO_KEY (30 requests per hour, 50 per day) with backoff. DONKI sometimes returns 503 or is slow, so use an 8 second timeout and 2 retries with backoff.
FIRST print ONE real sample of each response (trimmed) and look at the REAL field names before writing code. Do not assume them. Expected, but verify: flares have classType, peakTime, linkedEvents; SEP events have eventTime and linkedEvents with activityID values.

1. src/data/donkiLink.ts: pure functions used by both the script and the app.
   - linkStorms(flares, seps) -> Storm[] (the Storm type from src/engine/types.ts). Link a flare to a SEP when either one lists the other in linkedEvents.
   - gapHours = (SEP time - flare peakTime) in hours. Keep as a particle storm if 0.25 <= gapHours <= 48. A flare with no linked SEP is a false alarm: leave gapHours undefined.
   - tier for a particle storm: X-class = 'big', M-class = 'medium', anything lower = 'small'. For a false alarm: X = 'medium', else 'small' (tier does not matter for dose then). Write in DECISIONS.md that this refines the old rule, because dose now only happens when particles arrive.
   - id: the flare's own DONKI id. flareClass, flarePeak (ISO string), sepStart (ISO string) as returned. source 'saved' or 'live'.
2. scripts/fetch-donki.ts (run with npx tsx; allowed dev dependency tsx if not present). Fetch flares and SEP in chunks of at most 1 year (add a short delay) for: the last 3 years, plus May 2024 and October 2024. Print how many flares, SEPs and linked pairs you found. Save data/storms.json = { fetchedAt: ISO, source: 'NASA DONKI', storms: Storm[] } with ALL linked storms and up to 30 false alarms (M or X class), and copy it to src/data/storms.json.
   If fewer than 3 particle storms with a usable gap exist, widen to 2012-present and report. If still fewer than 3, STOP and tell me, do not invent.
3. Choose the 6 game storms and save them as src/data/gameStorms.json: 3 particle storms of different tiers (prefer May 2024 or October 2024 events if linked), 1 medium, 2 false alarms (X or M flare, no SEP). Never type a storm from memory; only pick from the fetched file.
4. api/donki.ts: a Vercel serverless function. GET /api/donki?type=FLR|SEP&start=YYYY-MM-DD&end=YYYY-MM-DD. It reads NASA_API_KEY from process.env, never sends the key to the browser, validates type and dates, uses an 8 second timeout, an in-memory cache for 1 hour, and sets Cache-Control s-maxage=3600. No extra dependency; use minimal local types.
5. src/data/loadStorms.ts: loadStorms(fetchImpl = fetch) -> { storms: Storm[], badge: { kind: 'LIVE' | 'SAVED', date: string } }. Try the live proxy for the last 90 days of FLR and SEP, link them with linkStorms, mark source 'live'. If it fails, times out (8 s) or returns no usable storms, use gameStorms.json with source 'saved' and badge SAVED with the fetchedAt date. The mission list is the 6 saved storms plus, when live works, the newest live storm labeled 'Latest from NASA'. Never label saved data as live.
6. src/data/facts.json: a list of { id, text, sourceName, sourceUrl, dateChecked }. Each text is 12 words or fewer per sentence. You must find a real source URL for every fact. Candidates to verify (leave out any you cannot verify on a NASA or NASA-partner page): Curiosity rover measured about 0.64 mSv per day of radiation on the Mars surface and about 1.84 mSv per day on the trip to Mars; the radio delay from Earth to the Moon is about 1.3 seconds one way; from Earth to Mars it is roughly 3 to 22 minutes one way; Mars gets less than half the sunlight Earth gets (find the real percentage); shelters help against solar storm particles but cosmic rays are hard to stop. Optionally one verified NASA OSDR finding with its study ID; skip if not verifiable.
7. Tests (src/data/*.test.ts): linkStorms with a small fixture built from the REAL sample you printed (say so in a comment); loadStorms with mocked fetch: live ok, live fails, live times out, live returns no storms; badge kinds; gap range filter (0.2 h and 60 h are rejected).

